-## Is the multiple fair?  _(sets the stance and the scenario multiples)_
+- **Consensus $1.37** sits between base ($1.29) and bull ($1.46), 46% of the way to bull. Getting there needs traffic of +2.9% at base-case margins, or a 24.37% restaurant margin at base-case revenue.
+- **The price implies** EPS of $1.14 at our base multiple of 27.0x (traffic of -3.7% at base margins), and 23.9x our base EPS. At our 27x base multiple, $30.90 implies EPS of $1.14, which at base margins needs traffic of -3.7%. That is worse than our bear case of -1.5%. So the market is either deeply discounting the Street's $1.37 or capping the multiple near 23x to 24x. Consensus at 22.6x next-FY EPS says the same thing from the other side: the price already embeds a large haircut to Street numbers. Our own 6% shortfall versus consensus is therefore probably priced, which is why being below the Street does not produce a short.
+- **Probability-weighted value $33.49**, +8.4% vs $30.90: **NO EDGE**.
+- **What would change the stance:** On price: below about $28, the $33.49 weighted value would sit roughly 20% above the price, enough to move toward a long without changing any driver. Above $34.85, our base target, the base case is fully priced and the weighted value turns negative, which would push toward avoid. On data at the Q3 print: a transaction comp of +1.5% or better with Q3 restaurant margin at or above 23.5% (down 100bp or less from 24.5%) would shift weight toward the bull case's +2.0% traffic and 24.62% margin. A negative transaction comp with margin below 23.0% would shift weight toward the bear case's -1.5% traffic and 21.88% margin. An FY2027 guide that keeps pricing below cost inflation would cut the bull probability, because the bull case depends on margin recovery.
